1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:06,462
Before anyone can chat with an AI assistant, your site has to reach an AI service.

2
00:00:06,462 --> 00:00:12,297
This video shows how connections, deployments and default models work together, why they

3
00:00:12,297 --> 00:00:14,780
exist, and how to set each one up.

4
00:00:19,380 --> 00:00:24,222
Every AI feature, such as chat, search over your documents, or a spoken

5
00:00:24,222 --> 00:00:28,120
conversation, needs a model that runs at an AI provider.

6
00:00:28,870 --> 00:00:31,483
Different jobs need different models:

7
00:00:31,483 --> 00:00:35,980
a chat model to answer, an embedding model to search, a vision model

8
00:00:35,980 --> 00:00:39,140
to read pictures, and speech models for voice.

9
00:00:39,890 --> 00:00:44,550
If every profile and feature kept its own address and key, changing a

10
00:00:44,550 --> 00:00:48,340
key or a model would mean editing each of them by hand.

11
00:00:49,090 --> 00:00:51,850
Instead, you set the provider up once.

12
00:00:51,850 --> 00:00:57,370
Everything else picks a model by name, and a change in one place reaches them all.

13
00:01:01,570 --> 00:01:06,973
A connection holds the address and the key of one account at an AI provider.

14
00:01:06,973 --> 00:01:10,860
The key is stored securely, and is never shown again.

15
00:01:11,610 --> 00:01:15,071
A deployment is one model you use through a connection.

16
00:01:15,071 --> 00:01:18,930
It declares what the model can do, which are its capabilities.

17
00:01:19,680 --> 00:01:24,586
The default deployments, in the site settings, say which model each kind of

18
00:01:24,586 --> 00:01:28,220
job uses when a profile or a chat does not choose one.

19
00:01:28,970 --> 00:01:33,116
Some connections and deployments are managed by your technical team.

20
00:01:33,116 --> 00:01:37,990
They appear in the same lists, marked Read-only, and you do not change them here.

21
00:01:42,179 --> 00:01:45,531
Open Artificial Intelligence, Provider Connections.

22
00:01:45,531 --> 00:01:51,059
The connections of the site are listed here, and below them the ones from configuration.

23
00:01:51,512 --> 00:01:53,172
Click Add Connection.

24
00:01:53,172 --> 00:01:57,724
The window lists one provider for each provider feature you enabled.

25
00:01:57,724 --> 00:01:59,692
Click Add on Azure OpenAI.

26
00:02:00,142 --> 00:02:02,020
Give the connection a Title.

27
00:02:02,020 --> 00:02:06,162
The Technical name is filled in from it, and cannot be changed later.

28
00:02:06,614 --> 00:02:12,225
Paste the Endpoint from your provider's portal, and choose how the site signs in:

29
00:02:12,225 --> 00:02:16,354
default authentication, a managed identity, or an API key.

30
00:02:16,869 --> 00:02:19,083
With an API key, paste it here.

31
00:02:19,083 --> 00:02:24,289
When you edit the connection later, leave the field blank to keep the stored key.

32
00:02:24,742 --> 00:02:25,727
Click Save.

33
00:02:25,727 --> 00:02:30,016
The site checks the fields, but it does not contact the provider, so

34
00:02:30,016 --> 00:02:33,262
a wrong key shows up the first time someone chats.

35
00:02:37,464 --> 00:02:42,044
Open Artificial Intelligence, Deployments, and click Add Deployment.

36
00:02:42,495 --> 00:02:45,295
Click Add on the provider of the model.

37
00:02:45,295 --> 00:02:47,535
Here it is Azure OpenAI again.

38
00:02:47,986 --> 00:02:51,308
Enter the Model name exactly as your provider knows it.

39
00:02:51,308 --> 00:02:56,046
The Technical name is filled in from it, and profiles and settings use that name.

40
00:02:56,498 --> 00:03:00,318
The Connection name is the connection this model is used through.

41
00:03:00,318 --> 00:03:03,438
It is already picked when the provider has only one.

42
00:03:03,892 --> 00:03:10,347
Under Trained features, a chat model keeps Text conversation, Tool calling and Streaming.

43
00:03:10,347 --> 00:03:11,502
Click Save.

44
00:03:11,953 --> 00:03:15,441
Add one deployment for every model the site needs.

45
00:03:15,441 --> 00:03:19,613
The next one is an embedding model, which searches documents.

46
00:03:20,063 --> 00:03:25,976
An embedding model gets Text embedding and nothing else, so the chat features are cleared.

47
00:03:25,976 --> 00:03:27,023
Click Save.

48
00:03:28,093 --> 00:03:32,173
Both models are now listed, each with its capabilities.

49
00:03:36,366 --> 00:03:39,785
The capabilities decide where a model is offered.

50
00:03:39,785 --> 00:03:44,386
An embedding model shows up only where an embedding model is needed.

51
00:03:44,840 --> 00:03:47,447
Tick only what the model was trained for.

52
00:03:47,447 --> 00:03:51,385
A feature you leave unticked is never asked of the model, and one

53
00:03:51,385 --> 00:03:54,990
you tick falsely is sent to the provider, which rejects it.

54
00:03:55,442 --> 00:04:01,313
The list also covers images, speech to text, text to speech, audio, video,

55
00:04:01,313 --> 00:04:04,322
and Realtime for live voice models.

56
00:04:04,775 --> 00:04:07,825
Ticking Reasoning shows Reasoning effort.

57
00:04:07,825 --> 00:04:13,276
Narrow the supported values, from Minimal to Extra high, and pick the default.

58
00:04:13,276 --> 00:04:17,235
Higher effort is more careful, but slower and costlier.

59
00:04:21,438 --> 00:04:24,145
Open Settings, Artificial Intelligence.

60
00:04:24,145 --> 00:04:28,538
The Default Deployments section picks a model for each kind of job.

61
00:04:28,991 --> 00:04:34,580
The Default chat deployment answers chats and chat interactions that do not pick a model.

62
00:04:34,580 --> 00:04:36,051
Set this one first.

63
00:04:36,503 --> 00:04:41,512
The Default utility deployment does small side jobs, such as planning and working

64
00:04:41,512 --> 00:04:43,239
out what the user wants.

65
00:04:43,239 --> 00:04:45,023
A small, fast model fits.

66
00:04:45,476 --> 00:04:49,407
The Default embedding deployment searches documents and knowledge.

67
00:04:49,407 --> 00:04:53,446
Each list shows only the deployments that have the right capability.

68
00:04:53,899 --> 00:04:55,023
Click Save.

69
00:04:55,023 --> 00:04:58,529
Anything left on Default now uses these models.

70
00:05:02,729 --> 00:05:08,919
Chat, chat widgets and AI workflow tasks need a deployment with Text conversation.

71
00:05:09,669 --> 00:05:15,083
Answers from documents, data sources and web crawlers need Text embedding for the

72
00:05:15,083 --> 00:05:17,449
search index, plus a chat model.

73
00:05:18,199 --> 00:05:23,364
Reading pictures needs Image input, and creating them needs Image output.

74
00:05:23,364 --> 00:05:28,399
Dictation needs Speech to text, and reading aloud needs Text to speech.

75
00:05:29,149 --> 00:05:32,553
Spoken conversations use a Realtime deployment.

76
00:05:32,553 --> 00:05:37,755
Without one, a Cascaded Realtime deployment chains a speech to text, a chat

77
00:05:37,755 --> 00:05:41,609
and a text to speech deployment that you already have.

78
00:05:43,609 --> 00:05:48,997
Set up a connection once, add a deployment for each model, and choose the defaults:

79
00:05:48,997 --> 00:05:53,054
every AI feature on your site then works from the same place.

80
00:05:53,054 --> 00:05:54,629
Thanks for watching.

